thread:
alex: Seeing intermittent JSON.parse failures in node sample-app after reconnect. Seems like an empty `data:` heartbeat gets glued into the next data block when chunks reassemble. Repro attached.

rui: Which sdk/sample versions?

alex: node-sdk v0.11.0, sample-app v1.6. Repro on mac + CI linux. Browser SDK not hitting it.

alex: Minimal raw stream:
```
data: {\"t\":\"one\"}

data:

data: {\"t\":\"two\"}

```

alex: After naive reassembly we sometimes get `{\"t\":\"one\"}\n{\"t\":\"two\"}` as a single `data:` payload.

jamie: Likely the `.split('\n\n')` + `.join('')` pattern. If an empty `data:` is split across buffers, joining removes the separator.

rui: Fix: normalize CRLF->LF, assemble `data:` lines with `join('\n')`, and treat whitespace-only assembled data as heartbeat (ignore). Also add unit tests that split between `data:` and payload and CRLF vs LF.

alex: Quick patch I used locally:
```js
buf = buf.replace(/\r\n/g, '\n');
const evs = buf.split('\n\n');
buf = evs.pop()||'';
ev(stuff).forEach(e=>{
  const data = e.split('\n').filter(l=>l.startsWith('data:')).map(l=>l.slice(5)).join('\n');
  if (!data.trim()) return; // heartbeat
  handle(JSON.parse(data));
});
```

jamie: Good. We'll make a canonical `parseEventStream` util in node-sdk and add a fuzz test in sdk-stream-tests.

